body:
This looks like a difference in [provoking vertex](https://www.khronos.org/opengl/wiki/Primitive#Provoking_vertex) definition (which determines the source of `flat` varying interpolation) between [OpenGL (last vertex) and Vulkan (first vertex)](https://registry.khronos.org/vulkan/specs/latest/man/html/VK_EXT_provoking_vertex.html#_description).

It looks like both [Vulkan](https://registry.khronos.org/vulkan/specs/latest/man/html/VK_EXT_provoking_vertex.html) and [OpenGL](https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL-Refpages/gl4/html/glProvokingVertex.xhtml) have extensions for changing the provoking vertex, but in this case I'm not sure that is necessary.

For example, here is a shader that produces ~identical outputs in both Compatibility and Forward+ by manipulating the clipping logic in the fragment shader instead of relying on the vertex interpolation:
```GLSL
shader_type canvas_item;

varying vec2 vertexPosition;

void vertex() {
	vertexPosition = (MODEL_MATRIX * vec4(VERTEX, 0.0, 1.0)).xy;
}

void fragment() {
	const vec2 tileSize = vec2(64.0, 64.0);
	const vec2 tileCount = vec2(10.0, 8.0);
	vec2 tile = floor(vertexPosition / tileSize);
	COLOR = vec4(tile / tileCount, 0.0, 1.0);
}
```
Compatability:
![compatability](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b61757fd-7d68-45e4-8651-28cb9260c57c)
Forward+:
![forward+](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d40a3e00-5beb-4493-992e-fdb04295173a)

Or, to match your original intent:
```GLSL
shader_type canvas_item;

uniform vec2 globalMousePosition;
uniform vec2 tileSize;

varying vec2 vertexPosition;

void vertex() {
	vertexPosition = (MODEL_MATRIX * vec4(VERTEX, 0.0, 1.0)).xy;
}

void fragment() {
	ivec2 tile = ivec2(floor(vertexPosition / tileSize));
	ivec2 mouseTile = ivec2(floor(globalMousePosition / tileSize));
	bool isHovered = (mouseTile == tile);

	vec4 tex = texture(TEXTURE, UV);
	COLOR = mix(tex, vec4(0.7, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0), 0.7 * float(isHovered));
}
```
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/77cd2db8-5810-41c1-9863-f79342cfa538)

The original issue of provoking vertex being different depending on API does make `flat` varyings a whole lot less useful though, so it may be worth looking into this further. Ideally we would use `glProvokingVertex` to bring OpenGL in line with the other APIs, but I'm not sure on the availability/coverage of that extension in GLES3.
